 "location": {
  "name": "Victoria Falls",
  "wiki": "Victoria_Falls",
  "country": "Zambia / Zimbabwe",
  "region": "Zambezi River, southern Africa",
  "lat": -17.9243,
  "lon": 25.8572,
  "tagline": "A mile-wide curtain of the Zambezi, and spray you can see from 30 miles away.",
  "intro": "Victoria Falls is where the Zambezi River drops into a narrow chasm on the border of Zambia and Zimbabwe, forming the largest sheet of falling water on Earth. Locals call it Mosi-oa-Tunya, \"the smoke that thunders\", for the towering column of mist that rises above it.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Width",
    "value": "1,708 m (5,604 ft)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Height",
    "value": "108 m (355 ft)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Mean flow",
    "value": "1,088 m³/s"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1989"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "The spray usually climbs more than 400 m into the air and can be seen from as far as 48 km (30 mi) away.",
   "On full-moon nights the mist can form a lunar rainbow, or \"moonbow\".",
   "The zigzag Batoka Gorges below the falls each mark an earlier position of the waterfall, which has been eating its way upstream for at least 100,000 years.",
   "In the low-water months (roughly September to December), swimmers can sit in Devil's Pool on Livingstone Island, a few feet from the edge of the drop.",
   "The falls split the Zambezi's fish life: 89 species live above them but only 39 below."
  ],
  "bestTime": "March to May for the full thundering flood (peak around April, though spray can hide the view); September to December for clear views and Devil's Pool.",
  "gettingThere": "Fly New York to Johannesburg (nonstop, about 15–16 hours), then connect to Victoria Falls Airport (Zimbabwe) or Livingstone (Zambia), each a short drive from the falls.",
